course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 26
title: Friendly name-calling and real insults (meanings only)
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Kids everywhere call each other names, and the same word can be a joke or a punch: "durnyk", dummy, said laughing between friends is fine; said angrily it hurts, and to an adult it is never okay.
explain: Every Ukrainian kid knows the comeback: "sam takYi!" — you're one yourself! (a girl says "sama taka!").
explain: Some names are gentle (bAlakun, chatterbox), some sting (zhadIba, greedy guts; yabeda, snitch; plaksa, crybaby), and some are real insults: duren (fool), and animal names like kozel (goat, meaning jerk).
explain: Older kids use street slang like "lokh" or "loshara", sucker, loser — learn what it means so you understand, but don't use it, especially at Sashko's house.
explain: The rule is tone: a grin and a mate's shoulder bump make a tease; an angry face makes an insult.
fact: "durnyk" means "silly, dummy"; among friends it is said laughing, but said angrily it hurts, and it is never said to adults.
fact: "sam takYi!" means "takes one to know one!", the standard comeback; a girl says "sama taka!".
fact: "duren" means fool or idiot; between close friends it can be a joke, but in anger it is a real insult and never said to an adult.
fact: "tormoz", literally "brake", means a slowcoach, someone slow to get a joke; it is mildly mean but fine between pals.
fact: "bAlakun" means chatterbox and is a gentle tease.
fact: "zhadIba" means greedy guts, someone who won't share; it stings a bit and kids use it a lot.
fact: "yabeda" means telltale or snitch, someone who tells the teacher; kids hate being called it.
fact: "plaksa" means crybaby; it is unkind, especially to someone really upset.
fact: "boyahuz" means scaredy-cat, used in daring games, and the usual answer is "ya ne boiusya!", I'm not scared!
fact: "lokh" and "loshara" are rude street slang for sucker or loser; parents dislike them.
fact: "kozel" means goat, and as an insult it means a jerk; using animal names (korOva, svynya, kozel) for people is rude.
fact: "ty shcho, durnyi?" means "are you crazy? are you stupid?"; depending on the tone it can be playful or offensive.
exercise: Sashko grins and says "durnyk!" after you trip over your own feet. What is he doing? | Teasing you in a friendly way | Seriously insulting you in front of everyone | Calling you clever | Asking if you're hurt
exercise: Someone calls you "durnyk". What is the standard comeback? | sam takYi! | DYAkuyu! | proBAch! | ya znAyu!
exercise: What would a girl say instead of "sam takYi!"? | sama taka! | sam takA! | sami taki! | samA takYi!
exercise: What does "duren" mean? | Fool, idiot | Friend, buddy | Dear, darling | Brave boy
exercise: Sashko takes ages to get the joke and his cousin says "tormoz!". What does it mean? | Slowcoach | Liar, fibber | Crybaby, wimp | Snitch, telltale
exercise: What is a "bAlakun"? | A chatterbox | A show-off with new toys | A thief | A slowcoach
exercise: You won't share your crisps and Sashko says "zhadIba!". What is he calling you? | Greedy guts, meanie | Fatty | Liar | Scaredy-cat, a coward
exercise: A boy told the teacher who broke the window. The others call him "yabeda". What does it mean? | Telltale, snitch | A hero, a brave boy | Crybaby, a whiner | Chatterbox, a talker
exercise: Why should you not say "plaksa" to someone really upset? | It means crybaby and is unkind | It means "get well soon", said kindly | It means "hurry up" | It is a word only for babies
exercise: Sashko won't climb the tall slide and his mate says "boyahuz!". What's the reply? | ya ne boiusya! | DYAkuyu! | sam takYi! | ne plAch!
exercise: Older kids call someone "loshara". What does it mean? | Sucker, loser (rude slang) | Lucky one | Little horse | Best friend, the one you trust most
exercise: Should you use "lokh" at Sashko's house? | No, it's rude slang parents dislike | Yes, it's a polite word for a friend | Yes, grannies love it | Only at dinner
exercise: An older kid yells "kozel!" at someone. What does he mean? | Jerk, as an insult | Goat, as a compliment | Strong, like a goat | Funny, a joke word
exercise: Sashko's mate says "ty shcho, durnyi?" with a laugh after a silly idea. What does it mean? | Are you crazy? | Are you hungry? | What's your name? | Where are you going?
test: my mate thinks durnyk is always a horrible insult. right? | No, between friends laughing it's a friendly tease | Yes, always the worst insult | No, it means clever | Yes, and it's a swear word
test: A kid snarls "kozel" at Sashko's dad in the street. How serious is it? | A real insult, very rude | A friendly joke | A compliment about his beard | A word for "sir"
test: Sashko cries because his hamster died, and a boy says "plaksa!". What is true? | That's unkind: he's really upset | That's fine, it's just a friendly tease | It means "poor you" | It means "hurry up"
test: oi zhadIba, give us one. What does the kid want? | You to share | You to leave him alone | You to stop talking | You to say sorry to him
test: In a cartoon, a boy answers "durnyk!" with "DYAkuyu!". What would a real Ukrainian kid more likely say? | sam takYi! | molodEts! | DObryi DEN! | zhakh!
test: Your cousin thinks "tormoz" is a word for a fast runner. What does it mean? | A slowcoach, literally a brake | A fast runner, literally a rocket | A strong kid | A clever kid
test: Sashko's little brother talks nonstop at dinner and their dad laughs: "bAlakun!". How does it sound? | A gentle tease | A real, nasty insult | A punishment for talking | A rude swear word
test: Someone dares you to jump off a high wall and calls you "boyahuz". What's the smart move? | Say "ya ne boiusya" and still don't jump | Jump to prove them wrong | Cry and call them plaksa | Push them
test: Why do kids hate being called "yabeda"? | Nobody likes being called a snitch | It means they're ugly | It means they're stupid at school | It means they're poor and have no toys
test: Sashko's mum hears you say "lokh" and frowns. Why? | It's rude street slang she dislikes | It's a swear word about mums | It means "lazy" | She thinks you said "lock"
test: You ask "ty shcho, durnyi?" angrily to a kid you don't know. How might he take it? | As an insult | As a friendly joke | As a compliment | As a greeting
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "durnyk" in the speech of Ukrainian children? | Its force depends upon tone, being amicable in jest and hurtful in anger | It is invariably a grave insult | It is invariably a compliment | It is a formal title addressed to elders
exam: Identify the conventional retort to name-calling, as employed by a boy. | sam takYi! | sama taka! | DYAkuyu! | proBAch!
exam: It is given that persons are designated by the names of animals such as "kozel", "korOva" or "svynya". Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Such designation is rude and constitutes an insult | Such designation is affectionate, as among close friends | Such designation is formal and respectful | Such designation is a religious greeting
exam: Ascertain the meaning and standing of the slang terms "lokh" and "loshara". | "Sucker" or "loser"; rude, and disliked by parents | "Friend" or "comrade"; polite and approved | "Teacher" or "head"; formal and neutral | "Lucky" or "fortunate"; complimentary
